1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:07,500
Vinegar Syndrome: Is Your Acetate Microfilm at Risk?. What the British Library's own survey found

2
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:13,521
Vinegar syndrome is the chemical breakdown of cellulose acetate film base.

3
00:00:13,521 --> 00:00:22,680
It releases acetic acid, so a vinegar smell from a film can or box is usually the first sign, and it cannot be reversed once it starts.

4
00:00:22,680 --> 00:00:31,500
Only acetate-base film is affected: the standard safety film base from the late 1940s until polyester took over through the 1980s.

5
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:41,906
The reaction is autocatalytic: once acidity passes a threshold, decay accelerates on its own. A reel can show little change for decades, then deteriorate quickly.

6
00:00:41,906 --> 00:00:50,500
Later symptoms include buckling, shrinking, brittleness and crystalline deposits. Film in that condition is no longer reliably readable or copyable.

7
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:59,474
Condition: Good: no deterioration detected. Share of sampled reels: Just under half. Condition: Fair to good: deterioration has started.

8
00:00:59,474 --> 00:01:07,448
Share of sampled reels: Just over half. Condition: Actively deteriorating. Share of sampled reels: 0.2% (one film). Condition: Critical.

9
00:01:07,448 --> 00:01:09,500
Share of sampled reels: None found.

10
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:19,616
1. Test with A-D strips: acid detector strips from the Image Permanence Institute. 2. Prioritise by severity: worst-affected reels first. 3.

11
00:01:19,616 --> 00:01:24,500
Capture to a stable medium: archival polyester microfilm, digital, or both.

12
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:34,990
Is all microfilm at risk? No. Only acetate-base film is vulnerable. Polyester base does not show vinegar syndrome. Can it be reversed? No.

13
00:01:34,990 --> 00:01:46,612
Cold, dry storage slows it, and the content should be captured before the film becomes unreadable. How do I test a collection? Place an A-D strip in a sealed can or box.

14
00:01:46,612 --> 00:01:49,500
It changes colour with acetic acid vapour.

15
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:53,811
Get help reformatting at-risk microfilm. Talk to Micrographics Data about microfilming, microfilm scanning and archival polyester film.

